@@ -0,0 +1,200 @@
+# Forking Base locally
+
+We build against a local fork of Base because our contract reads USDC on Base at
+`0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`.
+
+## TL;DR
+
+```bash
+yarn fork FORK_URL=base   # start the fork  (NOT `yarn fork base`)
+yarn fork:check           # prove it is actually Base before you debug anything
+```
+
+## What the onboarding note gets wrong
+
+The note says:
+
+> Start the chain with `yarn fork base` ... Sanity-check it with `cast chain-id`
+> against your local node; if you get a chain id back, the fork is up and you're on Base.
+
+Both halves are wrong. `yarn fork base` gives you a fork of **Ethereum mainnet**, and
+`cast chain-id` cannot tell the difference.
+
+### 1. `yarn fork base` silently forked mainnet, not Base
+
+The `base` argument was thrown away. Before this change, `packages/foundry/package.json`
+had:
+
+```json
+"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}",
+```
+
+Two things go wrong here:
+
+- **`${1:-...}` does not do what it looks like it does.** The script body is run by a
+  shell that has no positional parameters set, so `$1` is always unset and
+  `${1:-mainnet}` always expands to the literal `mainnet`. Reproduced directly:
+
+  ```
+  $ sh -c 'echo "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"' base
+  make fork FORK_URL=mainnet
+  ```
+
+- **Yarn appends extra arguments to the end of the command**, it does not substitute
+  them. Confirmed with a throwaway script in this package (`"__probe": "echo ARGS-RECEIVED:"`):
+
+  ```
+  $ yarn __probe FORK_URL=base
+  ARGS-RECEIVED: FORK_URL=base
+  ```
+
+  So `yarn fork base` expanded to `make fork FORK_URL=mainnet base` — `base` landed as a
+  second *make goal*, not as the fork URL.
+
+Running the old command in this checkout shows exactly that:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork base
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+The stray `base` goal never surfaces an error either: `make` runs the `fork` goal first,
+`anvil` blocks forever, and `make` never gets as far as `base` (where it would have said
+`No rule to make target 'base'`). The mistake is completely silent on a working machine.
+
+`--fork-url mainnet` is not ignored — `anvil` resolves it as an alias from
+`packages/foundry/foundry.toml`:
+
+```toml
+[rpc_endpoints]
+mainnet = "https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/${ALCHEMY_API_KEY}"
+...
+base = "https://mainnet.base.org"
+```
+
+(You can see the resolution in the failure output: the request goes to
+`https://eth-mainnet.alchemyapi.io/v2/...`.)
+
+### Why the USDC reads come back empty
+
+You get a perfectly healthy local node — it just forks the wrong chain. Ethereum mainnet
+has no contract at `0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913`; that address is Base's
+USDC. (Mainnet USDC is `0xA0b8...eB48`.) So on the mainnet fork that address is a plain
+empty account: `eth_getCode` returns `0x`, and every `staticcall` into it hits an address
+with no code.
+
+That is why the failure looks like "the token isn't deployed" rather than an RPC error.
+The node is up, blocks mine, transactions succeed — reads of that one address just return
+nothing. The contract was never the problem.
+
+### 2. `cast chain-id` cannot detect this
+
+The `fork` target in `packages/foundry/Makefile` pins the chain id:
+
+```make
+# Start a fork
+fork: setup-anvil-wallet
+	anvil --fork-url ${FORK_URL} --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+`--chain-id 31337` **overrides** the forked chain's id. So `cast chain-id` returns `31337`
+whether you forked Base, forked mainnet, or ran a bare `yarn chain` with no fork at all.
+It is deliberately set that way so the frontend keeps working — `packages/nextjs/scaffold.config.ts`
+targets `chains.foundry` (31337).
+
+A `31337` back means "something is listening and speaking JSON-RPC". Nothing more. The
+teammate who ran the check and concluded the fork was healthy got a true answer to a
+question that does not distinguish the two cases.
+
+## The command that actually works
+
+```bash
+yarn fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Since `yarn` appends arguments, this reaches `make` as `make fork FORK_URL=base` and
+overrides the Makefile default. Equivalently, from `packages/foundry`:
+
+```bash
+make fork FORK_URL=base
+```
+
+Verified in this checkout:
+
+```
+$ yarn fork FORK_URL=base
+anvil --fork-url base --chain-id 31337
+
+$ yarn fork            # default still mainnet, unchanged
+anvil --fork-url mainnet --chain-id 31337
+```
+
+Any alias from the `[rpc_endpoints]` table in `foundry.toml` works as `FORK_URL`
+(`base`, `baseSepolia`, `optimism`, ...), as does a full URL.
+
+### Repo changes made to support this
+
+- `packages/foundry/package.json`: `"fork": "make fork FORK_URL=${1:-mainnet}"` →
+  `"fork": "make fork"`. The argument is no longer swallowed; a passed
+  `FORK_URL=...` now reaches `make`.
+- `packages/foundry/Makefile`: added `FORK_URL ?= mainnet` so the default that used to
+  live in the broken shell expansion is preserved.
+- Added a `fork-check` make target and `yarn fork:check` / `yarn foundry:fork-check`
+  scripts.
+
+> Heads up: `"verify": "make verify RPC_URL=${1:-localhost}"` in the same
+> `packages/foundry/package.json` has the identical `${1:-...}` bug and always uses
+> `localhost`. Left alone here since it's outside this issue, but it wants the same
+> one-line fix.
+
+## The check that would have caught this
+
+Because the chain id is forced to `31337`, the only reliable way to identify the forked
+chain is to read **state that only exists on that chain**. Base USDC is exactly that
+marker — it is the thing we depend on, and it has code on Base and on no other chain we
+might plausibly fork (it is absent on mainnet, and on Optimism that address is empty too).
+
+```bash
+yarn fork:check
+```
+
+which runs (`packages/foundry/Makefile`):
+
+```make
+fork-check:
+	@USDC=$$(cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url $(RPC)); \
+	if [ "$$USDC" = "0x" ] || [ -z "$$USDC" ]; then \
+		echo "FAIL: no code at Base USDC on $(RPC) -- this is not a fork of Base."; \
+		exit 1; \
+	fi; \
+	SYM=$$(cast call 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 "symbol()(string)" --rpc-url $(RPC)); \
+	echo "OK: Base USDC present, symbol()=$$SYM"
+```
+
+Or as a one-liner:
+
+```bash
+cast code 0x833589fCD6eDb6E08f4c7C32D4f71b54bdA02913 --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+```
+
+- non-empty bytecode → you are on a fork of Base
+- `0x` → you are not, regardless of what `cast chain-id` says
+
+Run against the wrong-chain node that produced this report, it fails loudly instead of
+silently passing:
+
+```
+$ cast chain-id --rpc-url http://127.0.0.1:8545
+31337                                        # the old check: "looks fine"
+
+$ yarn fork:check
+FAIL: no code at Base USDC on http://127.0.0.1:8545 -- this is not a fork of Base.
+$ echo $?
+2
+```
+
+`RPC` is overridable if you're not on the default port: `yarn fork:check RPC=http://127.0.0.1:8546`.
+
+The `symbol()` call is the second half of the check: presence of code proves the fork
+carries Base state, and `symbol()` returning `USDC` proves the address is the token we
+think it is rather than some unrelated contract.
